        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.086,
            0.847,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "？ Do the diagonals of a rhombus intersect at any particular angle? Reason out and/or experiment to figure this out!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.141,
            0.847,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": "**Deduction 10—What can we say about the angles formed by the diagonals of a rhombus at their point of intersection?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.192,
            0.492,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "In the rhombus GAME, we have $\\Delta \\mathrm{GEO} \\cong \\Delta \\mathrm{MEO} (\\mathrm{why?})$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.236,
            0.492,
            0.312
          ],
          "content": "So, $\\angle GOE = \\angle DOE,$ as they are corresponding parts of congruent triangles. As they add up to $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ } ,$ they should be $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ each."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.498,
            0.178,
            0.845,
            0.313
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.316,
            0.849,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "**Property 6:** Diagonals of a rhombus intersect each other at an angle of $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.363,
            0.293,
            0.385
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
